WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.520
Swine dirty talk. A motherly girlfriend&nbsp;
in more ways than one. Unwelcome visitors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.000
and selfies. These moments in&nbsp;
Frasier will make you say "yikes!"

00:00:10.354 --> 00:00:14.080
"God, I could throw you down on this&nbsp;
floor right now and – hi! Hello."

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.440
A little over halfway through the final season&nbsp;
of "Frasier," Laurie Metcalf becomes the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.120
actress to play Nanette Guzman — Frasier's&nbsp;
ex-wife with whom he shares a potent sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.280
chemistry. Nanette is best known as the popular&nbsp;
children's TV and stage personality Nanny G,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.620
and when she shows up in "Caught in the Act,"&nbsp;
Roz forces a reunion between her and Frasier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.000
in hopes of scoring tickets to Nanette's&nbsp;
performance for her daughter. Nanette is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.440
desperate for a tryst with Frasier,&nbsp;
but we soon learn she's remarried.

00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:47.934
"Donald here does, well, just about&nbsp;
everything for me. He's my manager, producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.122 --> 00:00:51.300
songwriter, publicist…Husband.&nbsp;
I was getting there."

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.280
On the night of her Nanny G performance,&nbsp;
Frasier goes backstage to tell her nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.260
can happen between them. Nanette manages to&nbsp;
seduce Frasier regardless, and they throw each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.420
other on a bed and begin to take things too far&nbsp;
... when the bed begins lifting off the ground.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.960
It turns out this is the&nbsp;
opening of Nanny G's show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.560
when she's supposed to emerge from&nbsp;
the bed to sing the first number. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.360
first Frasier — buck naked at this point —&nbsp;
hides under the covers while Nanette sings.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.860
Desperately, she shoves a costume under&nbsp;
the blankets that includes a baby bonnet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.320
and a diaper. Frasier emerges from the bed,&nbsp;
wearing the ridiculous and disturbing outfit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.660
and Nanny G announces in surprise that she has&nbsp;
a new baby brother. Frasier tries to dance his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.693
way off the stage, only to find Nanette's&nbsp;
angry husband waiting for him in the wings.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.300
The Season 7 premiere, "Momma Mia," introduces us&nbsp;
to a new love interest, and at first, Frasier is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.820
blind to the reason for her allure. While waiting&nbsp;
for a blind date at Cafe Nervosa, Frasier is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.800
smitten with the woman we soon learn is children's&nbsp;
author Mia Preston, played by Rita Wilson.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.020
In spite of an awkward and funny mix-up&nbsp;
at the coffee shop, Frasier and Mia hit it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.340
off. There's no immediately apparent reason&nbsp;
to feel at all strange about the pairing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.140
until Mia meets Niles, who reveals to&nbsp;
Daphne that Frasier's new girlfriend is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.421
the spitting image of their dead mother.

00:02:12.421 --> 00:02:14.950
"Does she remind you of anyone? Oh, 

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:19.320
you noticed that, too? Are you kidding? From the&nbsp;
minute I laid eyes on her. Even I can see it."

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.000
When Mia joins the family for a weekend in a&nbsp;
cottage, Marty agrees with Niles' observation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.200
and it makes things even weirder when&nbsp;
Mia acts motherly toward Frasier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.640
like offering to cut up his food for him.&nbsp;
Marty and Niles argue about whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.460
to say something to Frasier, but&nbsp;
when they watch a home movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.860
includes Frasier's actual mom and he sees the&nbsp;
resemblance for himself, they don't need to.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.800
Unfortunately, once Frasier sees the similarities,&nbsp;
it ends things between him and Mia. But it isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.420
the end of Rita Wilson's time on "Frasier."&nbsp;
Wilson reprises the role of Frasier's actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.381
mother as part of a dream sequence in&nbsp;
Season 9's "Don Juan in Hell: Part 2."

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.540
Frasier isn't known for an&nbsp;
affinity for physical labor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.560
and in Season 10's "Some Assembly&nbsp;
Required," we discover an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.120
and disturbing reason why he should&nbsp;
probably stay away from such endeavors.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.200
Frasier's co-workers at the station sign&nbsp;
him up for a program that builds houses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.820
for low-income families. When the house is&nbsp;
complete, Frasier makes a speech as if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.440
were singularly responsible for the entire&nbsp;
house's construction, however his anecdotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.480
reveal he was only allowed to perform the&nbsp;
most limited of tasks, like stirring paint.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.860
Regardless, Frasier develops a strange&nbsp;
attachment to the house and its new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.238
family. He visits them just after they move&nbsp;
in and offers some free decorating tips.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.309
"Frasier, hi. What a surprise.&nbsp;
Honey, guess who's here again."

00:03:42.309 --> 00:03:46.800
The family is grateful at first, but&nbsp;
their gratitude grows sour as Frasier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.600
not only won't stop dropping in uninvited&nbsp;
but continues to criticize their decorating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:57.120
choices. He gives them tons of unasked&nbsp;
for accessories and acts betrayed when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.380
discovers they've put up a cow-shaped mailbox&nbsp;
rather than the one he bought them as a gift.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.300
The new homeowners are forced to eventually&nbsp;
shove him out of their front door so that he'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.960
get the message. He does eventually&nbsp;
come to his senses and apologize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.860
and they reward him with a tour&nbsp;
of their fully decorated home.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:20.280
Frasier is barely able to contain shrieks of&nbsp;
horror when he discovers the new black-and-white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.160
cow theme of the interior, along with a freshly&nbsp;
painted bathroom that Frasier describes with,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.193
"Wow, that's purple!"

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.460
In the final season of "Frasier,"&nbsp;
Julia Sweeney guest stars as Ann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.360
an insurance claims adjuster that&nbsp;
Roz sets up with Frasier. In "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.960
Placeholder," Roz knows things won't&nbsp;
work out between Ann and Frasier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.400
but she acts as matchmaker anyway to keep&nbsp;
Frasier's dating skills from wasting away.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.420
Frasier wants no part of it at first,&nbsp;
but after an awkward night of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.500
the fifth wheel at a couples' dinner&nbsp;
and another night of cat-sitting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.260
makes him feel like an old maid,&nbsp;
he's willing to take the plunge.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.400
Along with just the cringey notion&nbsp;
of dating someone as a placeholder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.660
Frasier's treatment of Ann doesn't make&nbsp;
him appear to be much of a gentleman. He is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.920
clearly annoyed by Ann right away and isn't&nbsp;
looking forward to the rest of the evening.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.460
When Ann heads to the restroom,&nbsp;
Frasier's co-worker, Kenny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.360
shows up with his lovely cousin,&nbsp;
Liz Wright — making her, literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.800
"Miss Right." Frasier lies to them, saying&nbsp;
his dinner with Ann is just business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.180
and he spends the rest of the date trying to&nbsp;
ditch Ann so he can take his shot with Liz.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.220
Of course, both Ann and Liz eventually figure&nbsp;
out what Frasier's up to, and he gets the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.000
shaming he deserves, though absurdly, Ann still&nbsp;
wants to meet him for coffee the next morning.

00:05:35.141 --> 00:05:38.188
"You're just another selfish, dishonest&nbsp;
creep. You're right. I don't know what to say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:42.828
Well, maybe you can come up with&nbsp;
something before we have coffee tomorrow."

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.080
Frasier uses the line "I'm listening" when he&nbsp;
takes calls on his radio show. This episode from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:55.864
Season 11 uses the line for its title, and sees&nbsp;
the good doctor eavesdrop at all the wrong times.

00:05:55.864 --> 00:06:00.404
"Now I know why you're always saying,&nbsp;
"I'm listening." Because you always are."

00:06:00.404 --> 00:06:04.380
Early in the episode, he accidentally&nbsp;
overhears Wendie Malick's Ronee — Marty's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.860
new love interest — making a date&nbsp;
with another man on the phone. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.340
he winds up hiding in the kitchen when&nbsp;
Marty confronts Ronee about the date.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.840
But without a doubt, the most cringey&nbsp;
snooping comes at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.400
episode. Frasier is searching Ronee's car for&nbsp;
a lost money clip when she and Marty show up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:24.780
appearing to be on the brink of breaking&nbsp;
up. Frasier hides in the back seat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.640
listens to them make up and, eventually,&nbsp;
agree to date each other exclusively.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.300
For a second, it looks like Frasier&nbsp;
will get a chance to escape when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.020
couple starts to head upstairs, but&nbsp;
then Ronee has a "better idea" about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.460
driving to a secluded beach so she&nbsp;
and Marty can "steam up the windows."

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.320
Frasier throws himself down on the back&nbsp;
seat before either of them can spot him and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.020
we can only assume, unwillingly goes along&nbsp;
for the ride where he's likely forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.220
know much more about his father's&nbsp;
love life than he ever wanted to.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:03.480
As a show reaches its final season, it's only&nbsp;
natural for ideas to run dry. Such is the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:09.660
with "Frasier" Season 11. In Episode 17, "Coots&nbsp;
and Ladders," Frasier recounts his bad day in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.040
series of flashbacks while Niles anticipates&nbsp;
a crime Frasier has admitted to committing.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.840
Although it's not exactly the most entertaining&nbsp;
plotline of the series, one moment in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:25.260
will make you cringe. After a kleptomaniac calls&nbsp;
into his radio show and a beautiful woman turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:30.420
down his advances, Frasier finds himself helping&nbsp;
his elderly neighbor change her lightbulb.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:35.940
When he learns the 83-year-old woman is still&nbsp;
successfully dating, Frasier's bitterness comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.160
to a boiling point, and he pockets one of&nbsp;
the woman's trinkets. Frasier's over-the-top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:46.020
reaction is played for laughs as he and Niles&nbsp;
try to return the item. But it's not as funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:50.640
when you remember he's a grown, educated&nbsp;
man taking advantage of a trusting elder.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.620
The cringiest part of all arrives when&nbsp;
Frasier and Niles stumble into the woman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.000
surprise birthday party and have to talk&nbsp;
their way out of Frasier's theft. They go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.780
through many hoops trying to return the&nbsp;
item without anyone noticing. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.321
they succeed — but we're left cringing&nbsp;
at the two foolish Crane brothers.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:14.460
Frasier has a chaotic dating life. The snooty,&nbsp;
middle-aged psychiatrist can't seem to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.480
down a woman for the life of him. Although&nbsp;
he doesn't have much luck with the ladies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.980
he still has not given up hope. But&nbsp;
sometimes, that doesn't feel like a virtue:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.880
Many of Frasier's most cringe-worthy&nbsp;
moments are the result of him trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.900
to impress women. We'll give him an&nbsp;
A for his mostly respectful effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:36.780
but we will not forgive the cringey dirty talk&nbsp;
between him and his new girlfriend, Madeline.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:42.060
In Season 2's "Adventures in Paradise: Part&nbsp;
1," Frasier and Madeline take a romantic trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.620
to Bora Bora. He soon finds out they have&nbsp;
booked a room next to his ex-wife and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:52.140
new lover, which is awful enough on its own. But&nbsp;
surprisingly, it doesn’t come close to the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:58.018
the couple hops in bed together and indulges in&nbsp;
the worst kind of foreplay. Frasier exclaims,

00:08:58.018 --> 00:08:59.940
"I'm sweating like the pig&nbsp;
that knows he's dinner."

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.384
When Madeline leaves to freshen&nbsp;
up, Frasier makes everything worse,

00:09:04.384 --> 00:09:06.904
"I'll be right back. Oink."

00:09:06.904 --> 00:09:09.960
Madeline might be into the strange pig reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.517
but for everyone else, it's&nbsp;
become a suppressed nightmare.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.140
We love the Crane brothers for their&nbsp;
intellectual interests and expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.160
tastes,when they're not putting others down&nbsp;
because of them, anyway. But when the two get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.360
tickets to see the hottest play in town,&nbsp;
their excitement quickly fades after they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.100
realize the tickets were for an earlier time,&nbsp;
forcing them to scrounge at the last minute.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.420
They'll do whatever it takes to maintain&nbsp;
their high-class social standing — except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.669
wait in the cancellation line&nbsp;
like a couple of peasants.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.600
"The last thing I need is to have&nbsp;
someone see us loitering outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.198
here like gutter riffraff pathetically&nbsp;
scrounging for last-minute tickets."

00:09:46.198 --> 00:09:52.080
Their superiority complexes are often charming,&nbsp;
but in this case, they lean towards cringe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:58.020
It's uncomfortable to watch Frasier and Niles&nbsp;
desperately try to conceal their lack of prestige.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.960
Everyone wants to impress their&nbsp;
friends every once in a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.300
but it's in the Crane brothers' nature to take&nbsp;
things too far. In reality, it's likely their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:11.997
friends and the rest of high society could not&nbsp;
care less about Frasier and Niles' ticket status.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:15.660
Frasier and Niles don't have the&nbsp;
best relationship with their father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.440
Martin. Their combative dynamic is&nbsp;
first introduced in the pilot episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.500
when Frasier agrees to let his&nbsp;
injured father move in with him.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:26.640
Although they learn to make it work, their&nbsp;
distinctly oppositional personalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.260
constantly clash. This remains true&nbsp;
in Season 1's "Dinner at Eight."

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.220
After trying to take Martin to a fancy&nbsp;
restaurant that's obviously out of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.300
comfort zone, Martin treats his boys to&nbsp;
his own favorite restaurant. Frasier and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:44.520
Niles can't help but rag on the place for&nbsp;
its lower-class status. They're outright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.600
offended by the casual waitstaff&nbsp;
and steak-and-potato offerings.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:52.860
Despite Martin's attempts to humble&nbsp;
his sons, they don't let up on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:58.260
not-so-subtle insults. Martin is forced to&nbsp;
leave the situation. Instead of apologizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.520
Frasier and Niles make it their mission to&nbsp;
finish their food in their father's honor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:07.860
They even go as far as staying after closing,&nbsp;
forcing the waitstaff to wait for them to finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:13.920
the food they clearly despise. The entire&nbsp;
affair is enough to make anyone cringe.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:20.280
Oh, Daphne. It was only a matter of time&nbsp;
before you made "Frasier" fans cringe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:25.920
Throughout the show, Niles and Daphne experience&nbsp;
a will-they-won't-they relationship, with Daphne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:31.380
oblivious to Niles' true feelings. They finally&nbsp;
make things official at the end of Season 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:37.200
which leads to Season 9's "The First Temptation&nbsp;
of Daphne." Still new in their relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.360
Daphne feels a tinge of jealousy and&nbsp;
uncertainty when Niles' new patient is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.680
revealed to have a crush on him. Instead&nbsp;
of trusting Niles to treat this patient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:50.880
she goes behind his back and looks through&nbsp;
the patient's confidential medical file.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:55.320
Not only is it cringe-inducing to witness&nbsp;
her insecurities and lack of boundaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:00.300
it's genuinely shocking: What Daphne does&nbsp;
is a crime. We can't even say that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:04.920
learns her lesson, because after coming&nbsp;
clean, she faces no consequences for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:10.320
actions. Instead, she and Niles make&nbsp;
up, and the patient is left exploited.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:16.920
A young Zooey Deschanel guest stars as&nbsp;
Roz's wild and opinionated cousin Jen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.560
Season 10's "Kissing Cousin." She embodies the&nbsp;
cynical 20-something with just the right amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.180
of cringe and attitude. Jen isn't afraid&nbsp;
to speak her mind, even when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:32.220
disagreeing with Frasier about his beloved&nbsp;
Freud. Listening to her go off on tangents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:38.700
about how everyone and everything is inferior&nbsp;
to her unique tastes is intensely cringey. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:43.200
Roz gets sick of her constant partying and&nbsp;
tendency to put her down for getting old.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:49.680
Everyone feels alienated by Jen's behavior —&nbsp;
except for a certain station manager. Kenny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:54.360
feels inspired by Jen's spontaneous and&nbsp;
carefree view of the world. It's sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.500
to see Kenny gain confidence from Jen's&nbsp;
words, but watching them partake in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:03.120
make-out session is significantly less&nbsp;
so. Clearly, this is where the episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:09.013
gets its title from. Overall, it probably&nbsp;
would be better off without the creepy gag.

00:13:09.013 --> 00:13:12.553
"So, Seattle still loves the Walkman, huh?"

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.460
Frasier's AM radio show is one of&nbsp;
his greatest successes. He feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.460
good about himself when he helps the&nbsp;
people of Seattle with their problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:25.560
and is constantly recognized for his stardom.&nbsp;
He's put up on such a high pedestal, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:30.720
that when a stranger from the radio station's&nbsp;
focus group gives his show negative feedback in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:35.940
Season 3's "The Focus Group," Frasier goes&nbsp;
out of his way to harass the innocent man.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.360
Manu is a newsstand owner who&nbsp;
does nothing more than express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.780
his opinion when asked for it. He simply states,

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:43.800
"I don't like it."

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:49.440
It's a fair assessment, but Frasier gets&nbsp;
fixated on it. He goes out of his way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.300
track down Manu to better understand why&nbsp;
he said what he said. Manu is a good guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:58.980
and he feels betrayed by the radio station&nbsp;
for lying to him about the hosts listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:04.320
in. The last thing he wants to do is talk poorly&nbsp;
about someone else. In his search for answers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:09.900
Frasier ends up burning down Manu's&nbsp;
newsstand and livelihood. This lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:15.660
empathy and narcissistic behavior makes&nbsp;
for a seriously cringe-worthy scene.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:21.660
In the Season 5 premiere, "Frasier's Imaginary&nbsp;
Friend," the titular lead hits it off with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:26.160
supermodel Kelly Easterbrook on a plane trip&nbsp;
to Mexico. During their romantic weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.860
Kelly tells him she's in the&nbsp;
middle of a break-up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:31.980
professional football player&nbsp;
and asks him to be discreet.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:36.840
Back home, Frasier unintentionally convinces&nbsp;
his family and co-workers that he's gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:40.680
from being lonely to delusional. When&nbsp;
he confides in his father, brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:46.260
and Daphne who he's dating, they think he's lying&nbsp;
to avoid humiliation. It gets to the point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.520
his family pays a surprise visit to him in a&nbsp;
restaurant where he says he's meeting Kelly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:55.860
and because she has to leave dinner early for&nbsp;
an emergency, Niles and Marty find Frasier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.860 --> 00:15:01.200
eating alone and are more convinced than&nbsp;
ever that Kelly is as real as Santa Claus.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.620
Rather than simply telling Kelly&nbsp;
about the situation and asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:07.620
if he could take a picture of them&nbsp;
to offer as proof — or, better yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:12.720
just let the whole thing go — Frasier goes&nbsp;
down a disturbingly creepy path. One night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:18.360
Frasier waits until Kelly is sound asleep,&nbsp;
pulls the sheets down to reveal her shoulder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:24.660
and tries to take a selfie years before anyone&nbsp;
used the word. Predictably, Kelly wakes up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:29.520
once she realizes what Frasier's trying to&nbsp;
do, she tells him exactly what she thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:34.737
of what he's done. Considering his behavior,&nbsp;
it's tough to blame her for dumping him hard.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.840
In Season 3's "The Friend," Frasier is&nbsp;
disappointed to realize he's spent a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.520
years back in his hometown without forging&nbsp;
any new friendships. Against Roz's advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:50.040
he uses his radio show to look for new buddies,&nbsp;
and as his producer predicts, most of the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.840
who respond are cringier than the show's&nbsp;
cringiest episodes. But amid all the refuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.840 --> 00:16:01.260
there appears to be a possible gem — Bob Reynolds,&nbsp;
who sends Frasier a very sane, friendly fax.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:06.600
Frasier meets Bob for coffee, and at first,&nbsp;
things are going well. Frasier and Bob — played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:12.060
by Griffin Dunne — share a love of literature.&nbsp;
But that's about as far as their similarities go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:18.240
Along with revealing an annoying tendency to use&nbsp;
air quotes often and without any rhyme or reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:22.920
Bob spends the rest of the meetup boring&nbsp;
Frasier by talking about his obsession with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:27.060
barbecue. Frasier is about to tell Bob&nbsp;
he'd rather not share dinner with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.900
when Bob backs up from the table,&nbsp;
revealing he's in a wheelchair.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:35.880
Frasier eventually breaks things off, at&nbsp;
first telling Bob the truth. But when Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:41.280
says he wishes it was the wheelchair that turned&nbsp;
Frasier off — because that would make Frasier the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.660
"jerk" instead of Bob — the psychiatrist&nbsp;
lies and says the chair is the problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:51.240
which Bob uses to turn all the employees&nbsp;
and patrons of Cafe Nervosa against him.

